  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health: Climate and Health buy-in to C40 Cities Finance Facility (CFF)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Latin America and Caribbean Regional):  .The objective of this mechanism is to support vulnerable migrant populations in urban areas to improve access health and reduce maternal and child mortality.  In its second year, this buy-in to the Bureau of Resilience, Environment, and Food Security&apos;s (REFS) donor-to-donor agreement with the C40 Cities Finance Facility and GIZ will continue to build on activities implemented by the REFS Program for Local Urban Sustainability (PLUS). The C40 Cities Finance Facility (CFF) will continue to strengthen municipal government capacity for urban adaptation and resilience programming to address public health impacts from climate change among migrants and other vulnerable groups through technical assistance for developing finance-ready proposals and potential linkages to external financing. This activity will be implemented in Colombia and other countries TBD in the region. .This activity cuts across health and adaptation to climate change through an approach to improving health governance in response to extreme weather stressors, which contributes to the Agency&apos;s Climate Strategy. This activity also aligns with the global health strategic priorities to reduce maternal and child mortality, and with the Vision for Health Systems Strengthening 2030 that highlights the importance of addressing macro-level changes within the development landscape, such as climate change and urbanization. The activity&apos;s focus on ensuring access for vulnerable migrant populations contributes to the Joint Regional Strategy&apos;s Bureau Objective 4.4 to strengthen health and education systems and improve inclusive access as well as Bureau Goal 5: A Climate-Resilient Hemisphere..</narrative>
  </description>
